  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SPVO &#x2013; Kinazi Living Income &#x2013; Premium Pay the Sustainable Way</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The &quot;Kinazi Living Income &#x2013; Premium Pay the Sustainable Way&quot; project addresses income instability and sustainability challenges faced by smallholder coffee farmers in Kinazi, Rwanda. While coffee is an important source of income for over 1,050 households in the region, farmers often earn below a living income: facing declining yields, rising production costs, and vulnerability to climate change. In addition Rwanda&apos;s zoning policy has been repealed, which has made farmer loyalty and consistent supply more difficult to secure by washing stations.This project brings together Dutch specialty coffee roaster Wakuli, Rwandan processor BAHO Coffee, and the non-gouvernmental organisation (NGO) AidEnvironment to identify and test practical pathways toward achieving a living income for farmers. Phase A focuses on conducting a local impact assessment that combines pricing analysis, agronomic diagnostics, and social risk mapping, including gender, child labour, and working conditions.The project aims to develop an action plan that links multiannual, premium pricing contracts to the adoption of regenerative farming practices, such as compost use, soil and water conservation, and bio-inputs. Through this model, farmers benefit from both financial stability and improved productivity.By combining value chain innovation with on-the-ground sustainability practices, this partnership seeks to offer a scalable model for responsible business in the global coffee sector &#x2013; proving that ethical sourcing can go hand in hand with environmental sustainability and commercial success.</narrative>
  </description>
